From 63c4346880e18bf02268bdfd0097426e8e4cdb6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>


Date: Mon, 26 May 2025 15:00:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] USERSTORY convert COUNT(column) to COUNT(*) if column
 is not nullable

(= FEATURE)

Example: assuming 'a' is a not nullable column:
  select count(a) from t1 where b>1;
is rewritten to
  select count(0) from t1 where b>1;
and, as a consequence, an index on 'b' is covering for the query,
which can speed up execution.

Implementation: when in fix_fields() for COUNT, if we see that the
optimization might be possible, we let fix_fields() run for the column
but tell it to not mark the column as used, yet. Next, we check if
it's nullable; if yes, we finally mark the column as used; if not, we
replace it with 0, and the nice effect in this case is that
table->covering_keys remains as it was before fix_fields() for COUNT:
it's like if 'a' had never been mentioned.

This works too if the column is from a derived table or view.

I didn't try to make it work with COUNT(non-nullable non-column
expression), like COUNT(a+0), because this is unlikely used and I
didn't want to open any possibility of bugs.

rds_simplify_count_not_null controls the feature.

Mtr testing: a new test is added; few other tests showed few changes
in EXPLAIN, I estimated that it was better to keep them unchanged,
that they didn't bring additional coverage (they are simple queries).
